    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a potent offensive outlook, anchored by two standout plays that maximize our efficiency targets. **Yordan Alvarez** (82.49) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (82.13) represent our highest-upside options; **Yordan Alvarez** benefits from an elite 0.476 xwOBA and a favorable matchup against Junk, while **Pete Crow-Armstrong** gains significant leverage from a 14% dynamic platoon boost and a lead-off spot that maximizes his elite speed\u2014a critical factor for our stolen base-starved roster. Further down the order, **Christian Yelich** (74.24) and **Colt Keith** (69.64) round out our most reliable starters, with **Christian Yelich** specifically benefiting from a slow delivery matchup that projects well for potential swipes.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision today centered on **Pedro Ramirez**, whose 50.46 score fell just short of the cut. While **Pedro Ramirez** benefits from wind conditions and park factors, the superior ceiling offered by **Junior Caminero**\u2014despite a challenging matchup against the high-velocity arm of Cease\u2014edged him out for the final Utility spot due to a more favorable lineup position. We have elected to bench **Chase Meidroth** (34.47) entirely, as the matchup against deGrom presents a sub-floor risk that contradicts our goal of preserving game caps for high-efficiency opportunities.\n\nRisk management remains a priority given the volatility of today\u2019s pitching slate. While **Junior Caminero** is locked in, owners should monitor his plate discipline against Cease, as the 19% reduction in SP skill suggests a high probability of strikeouts. Similarly, **Dillon Dingler** (50.27) sits at the low end of our starting threshold; while his wind-aided park boost keeps him in the lineup, he remains our most vulnerable asset if pinch-hitting scenarios arise. We remain confident in this configuration to capitalize on our stolen base needs while maintaining the offensive pressure required to climb the Roto standings.",
